  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "This weapon burned on water and nobody can rebuild it. Constantinople, the 670s. Fire pumped from bronze tubes on the prows of warships. Seawater did not touch it.",
        "visual_query": "byzantine constantinople sea walls",
        "visual_fallback": "stone walls"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A refugee architect called Kallinikos brought it to the emperor around 672. Arab fleets at the walls caught light and kept burning.",
        "visual_query": "medieval galley wooden warship",
        "visual_fallback": "wooden ship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Seven centuries of use, and not one usable recipe survives. Why? Because no single operator was ever told the whole system.",
        "visual_query": "byzantine manuscript illumination fire",
        "visual_fallback": "old manuscript"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Bulgarians captured 36 of the tubes and a stock of the substance in 814. They never managed to fire any of it.",
        "visual_query": "medieval bronze siege device",
        "visual_fallback": "bronze metal"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Scholars now read it as petroleum thickened with resin. A full-scale rebuild in 2002 threw flame fifteen metres, above a thousand degrees.",
        "visual_query": "controlled oil fire on water",
        "visual_fallback": "flame water"
      }
    ],
