      {
        "claim": "Byzantine Greek fire burned on water and was projected through bronze tubes called siphons mounted on warships, especially near the prow.",
        "segment_index": 0,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "Wikipedia: the main method was projection through a tube called a siphon mounted on ships, especially near the prow, and it burned on water; the World History Encyclopedia gives spraying under pressure from bronze tubes with a siphon pump and swivelling nozzle, and says water had no effect on it.",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "claude-research",
        "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_fire"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Theophanes the Confessor credits Greek fire to Kallinikos, an architect who fled to the Romans, around 672, and it was used against Arab fleets at the siege of Constantinople of 674-678.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "Wikipedia dates development to c. 672, credited by Theophanes the Confessor to Kallinikos, an architect who fled to the Romans, and gives first use at the siege of Constantinople of 674-678 against Arab fleets. The World History Encyclopedia dates first use to 678 CE in that siege, so the narration says 'the 670s'.",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "claude-research",
        "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_fire"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Knowledge of Greek fire was compartmentalised as a state secret, so no single operator knew the whole system, and the composition was eventually lost.",
