        "note": "86% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Bulgarians captured 36 siphons and a quantity of the substance in 814 and were unable to use them.",
        "segment_index": 3,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "88% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Most modern scholars hold that Greek fire was petroleum-based with added resins, and John Haldon's 2002 full-scale test with crude oil and wood resins produced a flame above 1,000 degrees Celsius with a range of up to 15 metres.",
        "segment_index": 4,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "Wikipedia records the modern scholarly consensus of a petroleum base mixed with resins, comparable to napalm, and Haldon's 2002 full-scale test reaching a flame temperature above 1,000 C with a range of up to 15 metres using crude oil and wood resins. The World History Encyclopedia gives 10-15 metres for a Haldon replica, so fifteen metres is the upper figure both report.",
        "correction": null,
